course: books
lesson: 63
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter X, part 3: The Further Adventures of Toad (the most conceited song and the very same motor-car)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Full of stew and with money in his pocket, Toad marches along with his chin in the air, telling himself the story of his own cleverness: prison, train, canal, horse — he has beaten them all! He makes up a song in praise of himself and sings it at the top of his voice, though nobody is there to hear. The book calls it «perhaps the most conceited song that any animal ever composed».
explain: «But his pride was shortly to have a severe fall.» On the high road a speck comes towards him, becomes a dot, then a blob, then something very familiar — a motor-car, with its double «poop-poop». Toad is thrilled: he will tell them a story, get a lift, and perhaps even drive up to Toad Hall in a car — «one in the eye for Badger!»
explain: Then his heart turns to water. It is the very car he stole from the yard of the Red Lion, with the very same people in it! He collapses in a heap in the road, groaning at what a fool he has been to strut about singing instead of hiding till dark.
explain: But the gentlemen don't recognise him. They think he is a poor old washerwoman who has fainted from the heat, and kindly lift him into the car. Toad opens one eye, then the other — and soon he is asking to sit in the front seat «for the fresh air», right beside the driver, as the old craving creeps over him again — and soon he will be begging to drive.
fact: Toad boasts to himself: «My enemies shut me up in prison... I walk out through them all... I am The Toad, the handsome, the popular, the successful Toad!»
fact: He tells it all as cleverness: he even calls the barge-woman «a woman fat of body and very evil-minded», though she had been kind until he lied and insulted her.
fact: The song begins: «The world has held great Heroes, / As history-books have showed; / But never a name to go down to fame / Compared with that of Toad!»
fact: «The clever men at Oxford / Know all that there is to be knowed.» «Knowed» is wrong English (it should be «known»); Toad uses it just to rhyme with «Toad».
fact: In other verses Toad says he was the one who told the animals in Noah's Ark «There's land ahead», that the army saluted him instead of the King or Kitchener (a famous British general of that time), and that the Queen called him «that handsome man».
fact: «There was a great deal more of the same sort, but too dreadfully conceited to be written down. These are some of the milder verses.» (milder = less extreme)
fact: «He sang as he walked, and he walked as he sang, and got more inflated every minute.» (inflated = puffed up, like a balloon)
fact: On the high road he sees «a speck that turned into a dot and then into a blob, and then into something very familiar».
fact: Toad plans to «pitch them a yarn» (tell them a made-up story) and calls the drivers «my brothers of the wheel»; driving home would be «one in the eye for Badger» (a nasty surprise that proves Badger wrong).
fact: It is the very car he stole from the yard of the Red Lion Hotel, with the very same people he had watched at lunch in the coffee-room.
fact: Toad groans: «What did I want to go strutting about the country for, singing conceited songs... instead of hiding till nightfall and slipping home quietly by back ways!»
fact: The gentlemen say: «Here is a poor old thing—a washerwoman apparently—who has fainted in the road!» They lift him into the car and prop him up with soft cushions, meaning to take her on to the nearest village, where she probably has friends.
fact: Toad asks to sit «on the front seat there, beside the driver, where I could get the fresh air full in my face»; the gentleman says «What a very sensible woman!»
fact: In the front seat the old cravings rise up again: «It is fate!» he said to himself. «Why strive? why struggle?» (strive = try hard)
fact: Talk in this lesson: «rizz» = charm; «salty» = annoyed; «wicked» (New England) = very; «aye» (Scotland) = yes; «bruv» (London) = mate.
exercise: Why was Toad marching along with his chin in the air? | He was proud of all his escapes and thought himself very clever | His neck was stiff from sleeping in the tree | He was looking up at the larks singing high over the fields | He was trying to see over the tall hedges to find the river
exercise: What does Toad say he did to the prison guards? | «I walk out through them all» | He fought them all with a stick | He paid them | He sang to them every night until they fell asleep and let him out
exercise: How many people heard Toad's song? | Nobody but himself | The gipsy by his fire | Some rabbits by the road | A crowd of travellers
exercise: «The world has held great Heroes, As history-books have showed.» Whose name, says the song, is greater than all of them? | Toad's | The King's | Badger's | The engine-driver's, who helped him escape
exercise: «Know all that there is to be knowed.» What is wrong with «knowed»? | It should be «known»; Toad bends the grammar to rhyme with «Toad» | Nothing at all, it is perfectly good English that everyone at Oxford uses | It should be «knew» | It is a word in French
exercise: In Toad's song, who said «There's land ahead» in the Ark? | Toad himself, he claims | Noah, standing on the roof of the Ark with his telescope | The Rat, looking out from his boat | A dove flying back with a leaf
exercise: «Was it the King? Or Kitchener?» Who does the song say the army saluted? | Mr. Toad | The King in his crown | Kitchener, the famous general | The Chief Weasel in his best coat
exercise: Who, in the song, called Toad «that handsome man»? | The Queen | The gaoler's daughter | The barge-woman | The engine-driver's wife, who did his washing
exercise: Why does the book say it did not print the rest of the song? | It was too dreadfully conceited to write down | It was far too long to fit on one page of the book | It was lost | Toad forgot it
exercise: «He sang as he walked, and he walked as he sang, and got more ...» what? | Inflated | Tired | Hungry | Quiet and sad, as he thought of prison
exercise: «But his pride was shortly to have ...» what? | A severe fall | A good long rest | A prize for singing | A song of its own, sung by all the animals
exercise: What did Toad see coming on the high road? | A speck, then a dot, then a blob, then a motor-car | A barge | A troop of soldiers with a band, marching towards Toad Hall with flags | The Rat in a boat
exercise: What did Toad call the people in motor-cars? | «My brothers of the wheel» | «Road-hogs» | «Villains» | «My dear friends of the river»
exercise: «That will be one in the eye for Badger!» What does Toad mean? | Badger would get a nasty surprise, proved wrong | Badger would hurt his eye | Badger would wink at him across the table at dinner, to show he was proud | Badger would come to the party
exercise: What did Toad plan to tell the people in the car? | A yarn — a made-up story | The whole truth | Nothing at all | Where the nearest police-station was
exercise: Why did Toad collapse in the road? | It was the very car he had stolen from the Red Lion | He was ill from eating far too much of the gipsy's rich stew | The sun was too hot | He tripped over his long gown
exercise: «O, what a fool I have been!» What does Toad wish he had done instead? | Hidden till nightfall and slipped home by back ways | Stayed safely in prison with the gaoler's daughter and her toast | Kept the horse | Gone back to the barge
exercise: What did the gentlemen think had happened to the «washerwoman»? | She had fainted, perhaps from the heat or hunger | She had been robbed of her washing money by a tramp on the road | She was drunk | She had been hit by a cart
exercise: Where did the gentlemen plan to take her? | To the nearest village, where she probably had friends | To the police-station in the town with the castle | To Toad Hall | To a doctor in the town with the castle
exercise: How did Toad find out he was safe? | He heard them talk kindly and knew he was not recognised | They told him he was free | He saw the policemen ride away in another car in the opposite direction | The car turned round
exercise: How did Toad open his eyes? | First one eye and then the other | Both at once, wide | He never did | Very slowly, after a whole hour of pretending to sleep
exercise: Why did Toad say he wanted the front seat? | To get the fresh air full in his face | To see the road | To talk to the driver | To be nearer the door in case he needed to jump out
exercise: «What a very sensible woman!» What did the gentleman not realise? | The «woman» was Toad, the thief of his car | That she was hungry | That she was a very good driver who had once driven a train | That the seat was broken
exercise: «It is fate! Why strive? why struggle?» What is Toad about to do? | Give in to his craving for motor-cars | Run away across the fields as fast as his short legs can go | Confess everything | Fall asleep
test: Finn: «Toad sang his song to a massive crowd, they went wild.» Put it right. | Nobody heard it — he sang to himself on an empty road | Right, the whole village came out | The barge-woman heard it and clapped | Only the horse heard it, and it neighed along to the tune
test: Why is the line about Oxford funny? | Toad says he is cleverer than the clever men at Oxford, but uses the wrong word «knowed» | Because Oxford is a river and the clever men are fish | Because Toad went to Oxford and passed all the exams | It is not funny
test: What does «the most conceited song that any animal ever composed» tell us about Toad now? | His adventures have made him even more boastful, not wiser | He has become humble | He is a great poet, as the Rat always says, and better than the Rat | He is sorry
test: Leo, salty (annoyed): «the barge-woman was evil-minded, Toad said so.» Is Toad a fair judge? | No: she was kind until he lied, boasted and insulted her | Yes: she was cruel to him from the very first minute he stepped aboard | Yes: she stole his money | No: she was his aunt
test: What would have happened if Toad had hidden till dark, as he later wished? | He might have slipped home quietly without meeting the car | He would have been caught by the barge-woman | He would have frozen in the snow and been found by Badger in the Wild Wood | He would have missed the train
test: Why did the people in the car not recognise Toad? | He was in the washerwoman's dress and bonnet, lying in a crumpled heap | They had never seen a toad before in their lives, so they could not tell | It was dark and foggy | They had forgotten all about their car
test: True or false? «Toad stays humble in the car because he is frightened.» | False: once safe, he asks for the front seat and then to drive | True: he never says a single word all the way to the village | True: he gets out at the village | False: he jumps out at once
test: Aye (yes), Isla's wee brother says: «Toad's rizz (charm) won them over.» What really made the gentlemen kind? | They pitied a poor washerwoman who seemed to have fainted | Toad's handsome looks | Toad's song, which they heard from far down the road | Toad paid them
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, where did Toad first steal this motor-car? | From the yard of the Red Lion, while its owners ate lunch | From his own coach-house | From a garage in Town, the day after the caravan was wrecked | From the police
test: Looking back: in chapter II, what did Toad keep murmuring in the road after the first motor-car passed? | «Poop-poop!» | «Help, police!» | «My poor cart!» | «Stop, stop, stop!»
test: Looking back: in chapter VIII, what did Toad tell the engine-driver about the car? | That he «only borrowed a motorcar» while the owners were at lunch | That he bought it | That he had never even touched it, and the weasels took it | That the Chief Weasel stole it
test: Looking back: in chapter IV, what did Badger say he would do to Toad once winter was over? | Take him in hand and make him be a sensible Toad | Throw him in the river and leave him to swim home alone | Teach him to row | Give him a new car
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, who warned the Rat that Toad is «at his artfullest» when he seems quiet and good? | Badger | The Mole | The Otter | The gaoler
test: Which word best describes how Toad's mood changes in this part? | Proud, then terrified, then proud and tempted again | Sad all the way, from the first verse to the last | Calm, then angry | Sleepy, then hungry
test: ★★★ «A speck that turned into a dot and then into a blob.» Why does the car seem to grow? | It is driving closer, so it looks bigger | It is getting bigger by itself | Toad's eyes are tired after his night in the hollow tree | Fog is lifting
test: ★★★ Toad's song uses «showed», «knowed», «flowed», «road», «sewed». Which word do they all rhyme with? | Toad | Road-hog | Ode | Hoot
test: ★★★ In this part Toad says «O, what a fool I have been!» and a few minutes later asks to drive. What does this show about Toad? | His regrets last only while he is frightened | He has truly changed | He has simply forgotten that the car was ever stolen at all | He wanted to give the car back
exam: Identify the reason assigned by the narrator for the omission of the remainder of Toad's song. | That it was too dreadfully conceited to be written down | That it exceeded the length permitted | That the manuscript was lost in the canal together with Toad's bonnet | That Toad himself withheld it
exam: Ascertain the cause of Toad's collapse upon the high road. | His recognition of the motor-car as the very one he had stolen | Exhaustion consequent upon his journey | An injury sustained in the canal when he was thrown from the barge | Excessive consumption of stew
exam: It is given that the occupants of the car did not recognise Toad. State the condition they believed him to be in. | A washerwoman who had fainted | A fugitive from justice | A gentleman in disguise, fleeing from the police | A lost child
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises the pretext upon which Toad obtained the front seat. | That he required the fresh air full in his face | That he wished to learn to steer | That he was the lawful owner of the vehicle and entitled to the seat | That he suffered from travel-sickness
